---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Claim from '../../components/Claim.astro';
import MccChart from '../../components/MccChart.astro';
import MutationExplorer from '../../components/MutationExplorer.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import type { ClaimId } from '../../data/claims';

const sources: ClaimId[] = [
  'ob.libraries', 'ob.instances', 'ob.requirements', 'ob.baseRate', 'ob.f1.flagAll', 'ob.mcc.flagAll',
  'ob.mcc.b3', 'ob.ci.b3', 'ob.mcc.b4', 'ob.ci.b4', 'ob.mcc.p1', 'ob.ci.p1', 'ob.mcc.b5', 'ob.ci.b5',
  'ob.delta.b3', 'ob.delta.b4', 'ob.delta.b5', 'ob.deltaCi.b5', 'ob.recall.absent.p1', 'ob.recall.other.p1',
  'ob.extraction.cost', 'ob.extraction.mcc', 'ob.real.trajectories', 'ob.real.omitted', 'ob.real.fpr',
  'ob.precision.p1', 'ob.fpr.p1', 'ob.fpr.b5', 'ob.gate.precision', 'ob.gate.bar', 'ob.gate.fired', 'ob.gate.p95',
  'ob.tests', 'ob.unwired.n', 'ob.recall.absent.b5', 'ob.recall.unwired.b5', 'ob.recall.stub.b5',
  'ob.b7.auroc', 'ob.b7.ece', 'ob.b7.eceBar', 'ob.b7.delta', 'ob.b7.deltaCi',
];
const toc = [
  { id: 'question', label: 'The question' },
  { id: 'benchmark', label: 'How the benchmark is built' },
  { id: 'results', label: 'Results' },
  { id: 'against', label: 'What went against me' },
  { id: 'b7', label: 'A judge that reports a probability' },
  { id: 'gate', label: 'Shipping it as a gate' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'Limits' },
  { id: 'reproduce', label: 'Reproduce' },
];
---
<CaseStudy
  title="OmitBench"
  dek="A benchmark for silent omissions in coding-agent patches, and the measurements that didn't flatter it."
  description="A benchmark and deterministic detector for silent omissions in coding-agent patches. The detector lost to a mid-tier LLM judge, and shipped anyway as a precision-first CI gate."
  year="2026"
  stack={['Python', 'AST analysis', 'mutation testing', 'LLM-as-judge baselines', 'cluster bootstrap', 'GitHub Actions']}
  links={[
    { label: 'Code', href: 'https://github.com/Avnish1505/Omitbench' },
    { label: 'Write-up', href: 'https://avnish1505.github.io/omitbench-engineering-blog/' },
  ]}
  sources={sources}
  toc={toc}
  ogImage="/og/omitbench.png"
>
  <h2 id="question">The question</h2>
  <p>
    When a coding agent reports that it is done, part of what it planned can be missing from the diff. The patch applies,
    nothing errors, and the gap shows up later. I wanted to know whether a detector built only on program analysis could find
    those omissions better than an LLM judge, without ever seeing the reference solution.
  </p>

  <h2 id="benchmark">How the benchmark is built</h2>
  <p>
    I took real merged commits from <Claim id="ob.libraries" /> Python libraries: attrs, click, flask, httpx, itsdangerous, jinja,
    requests and werkzeug. For each commit I applied the full patch, then broke one newly defined symbol in one of three ways.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>ABSENT</code>: the definition is deleted and its call sites stay.</li>
    <li><code>UNWIRED</code>: the definition stays and every call to it is neutralised, so it reads as finished and never runs.</li>
    <li><code>STUB</code>: the definition and its calls stay, and the body is hollowed out.</li>
  </ul>
  <MutationExplorer />
  <p>
    That gives <Claim id="ob.instances" /> instances and <Claim id="ob.requirements" /> requirements scored per detector, at a
    base rate of <Claim id="ob.baseRate" />. The mutation defines the label, so ground truth costs nothing. No detector ever
    receives the gold patch: a leakage test plants a canary in it, and CI fails if the canary reaches a detector.
  </p>
  <p>
    I committed to MCC as the headline metric before running anything. At this base rate F1 rewards a detector that flags
    everything, which scores F1 <Claim id="ob.f1.flagAll" /> and MCC <Claim id="ob.mcc.flagAll" />.
  </p>

  <h2 id="results">Results</h2>
  <MccChart />
  <p>Every detector sees the same instances, so the comparison that matters is the paired difference, resampled by instance:</p>
  <math display="block" aria-label="Delta MCC equals MCC of P1 minus MCC of B, with a 95 percent interval from the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles over bootstrap resamples of instances">
    <mrow>
      <mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>MCC</mi><mtext>P1</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>MCC</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub>
      <mo>,</mo><mspace width="1em"></mspace>
      <msub><mtext>CI</mtext><mn>95</mn></msub><mo>=</mo>
      <mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0.025</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.3em"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0.975</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>]</mo></mrow>
    </mrow>
  </math>
  <p class="small muted">Each <em>Δ*</em> is the same difference on a resample of instances, not of requirements, because the requirements of one instance share a repository, a patch and a symbol. Resampling them one by one would pretend they are independent and give intervals that are too narrow.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">My detector against</th><th scope="col">MCC difference</th><th scope="col">95% interval</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Line grep, no AST</td><td><Claim id="ob.delta.b3" /></td><td class="win">excludes zero, I win</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Reasoning LLM judge</td><td><Claim id="ob.delta.b4" /></td><td class="win">excludes zero, I win</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mid-tier LLM judge</td><td><Claim id="ob.delta.b5" /></td><td class="lose"><Claim id="ob.deltaCi.b5" />, I lose</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The loss has a clear cause. My detector asks one question: is the symbol defined? That gives
    recall <Claim id="ob.recall.absent.p1" /> on <code>ABSENT</code> omissions and <Claim id="ob.recall.other.p1" /> on <code>UNWIRED</code> and <code>STUB</code>, by construction. The mid-tier judge recovers part of both, and on this corpus's
    mix that trade wins. The thesis I started with, that LLM judges are structurally weak at spotting omissions, is not
    supported for that judge.
  </p>

  <h2 id="against">What went against me</h2>
  <p>
    Two more elaborate detectors added reachability analysis. They flagged most correct public API symbols as missing and lost
    to plain grep. I allowed myself one fix, decided in advance and measured once. It narrowed the gap but did not clear the bar
    I had set, so both detectors were retired. Their code is still in the repository, marked as retired.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every requirement in the main table comes from the gold patch, which makes those numbers an upper bound. To measure the gap,
    I had an LLM read only the commit message or issue text and guess the requirements. That cost <Claim id="ob.extraction.cost" /> for all <Claim id="ob.instances" /> instances, and my detector's MCC fell from <Claim id="ob.mcc.p1" /> to <Claim id="ob.extraction.mcc" />, worse than flagging nothing. Extraction, not detection, is the
    hard part.
  </p>
  <p>
    I also collected <Claim id="ob.real.trajectories" /> real agent trajectories. Every agent succeeded, with <Claim id="ob.real.omitted" /> requirements omitted, so recall could not be measured at all. What did carry over was the false
    positive rate: <Claim id="ob.real.fpr" /> on real code against <Claim id="ob.fpr.p1" /> on the synthetic corpus.
  </p>

  <h2 id="b7">A judge that reports a probability</h2>
  <p>
    The mid-tier judge beats my detector, but it returns a bare label, so a gate built on it has nothing to threshold.
    I added B7: TypeSafe's Jev, a model that answers yes or no questions with a probability. Its makers describe those
    probabilities as calibrated and publish no calibration numbers, so I tested that on this task. The variants, the thresholds
    and the rule for "calibrated" were written down before the first call.
  </p>
  <p>
    It ranks well, with AUROC <Claim id="ob.b7.auroc" />. It is not calibrated: expected calibration error is <Claim id="ob.b7.ece" /> against
    a pre-registered bar of <Claim id="ob.b7.eceBar" />, and it predicts omissions more often than they happen in every probability bin.
    As a judge it loses to the mid-tier LLM: the paired MCC difference is <Claim id="ob.b7.delta" />, with an interval of <Claim id="ob.b7.deltaCi" />.
  </p>
  <math display="block" aria-label="Expected calibration error equals the sum over bins b of the fraction of items in bin b times the absolute difference between the observed rate and the mean predicted probability in that bin">
    <mrow>
      <mtext>ECE</mtext><mo>=</mo>
      <munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>b</mi></munder>
      <mfrac><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac>
      <mrow><mo>|</mo><mtext>observed</mtext><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>−</mo><mtext>predicted</mtext><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>|</mo></mrow>
    </mrow>
  </math>
  <p>
    High AUROC with high ECE is a useful combination to be able to name: the model orders cases correctly, but its 0.8 does not mean 80%.
    A gate that thresholds it would need recalibration first.
  </p>

  <h2 id="gate">Shipping it as a gate</h2>
  <p>
    The GitHub Action reads an explicit checklist of symbols from the pull request and comments when one is missing from the
    diff. It uses my detector only, with no LLM and no network call. Requirements come from the checklist and are never guessed
    from prose, because the extraction experiment showed what guessing does.
  </p>
  <p>
    I shipped my detector, not the judge that beat it. A gate that cries wolf gets switched off, so precision was the ship
    criterion. Mine has precision <Claim id="ob.precision.p1" /> at a false positive rate of <Claim id="ob.fpr.p1" />, against <Claim id="ob.fpr.b5" /> for the mid-tier judge. At the last check its precision on the frozen corpus was <Claim id="ob.gate.precision" />, above the <Claim id="ob.gate.bar" /> bar.
  </p>
  <p>
    On a demo pull request with a deliberately missing symbol, the workflow commented in <Claim id="ob.gate.fired" />. Detection
    takes <Claim id="ob.gate.p95" /> per instance at p95, and the suite now has <Claim id="ob.tests" /> tests. That is one demo on my own
    repository. It shows the mechanism works, not that any team gets value from it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The omissions are injected, not observed. Until there are real traces that contain real omissions, none of this generalises to deployed agents.</li>
    <li>Python only, and only functions and classes. No config keys, dependencies or behaviour-only requirements.</li>
    <li><code>UNWIRED</code> has <Claim id="ob.unwired.n" /> instances. It needs about a hundred before that column means more than noise.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="reproduce">Reproduce</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" label="terminal" code={`git clone https://github.com/Avnish1505/Omitbench
cd Omitbench
make test        # unit tests and leakage guards
make reproduce   # regenerates every number from committed shards, no network or API key`} />
</CaseStudy>
